From e2c8ea66f5969bc6a5f1a157ada659c39a7630a9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 12:34:33 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 67/71] inline-3d(Phase 3): name the flat regions and wish them
 2D
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Phase 3 Stage 2, browser#88 — the BROWSER half of the per-region hardware
wish. The runtime half shipped as XR_DXR_weave spec v8
(displayxr-runtime#1065); this patch produces the one thing it needs from
the caller: the list of rects that are FLAT.

Until now the weave path drove the panel's physical 3D element
all-or-nothing. One woven element held the WHOLE panel behind the lens, so
the flat 2D around and over it was viewed through a lenticular it did not
want — the ghosting this browser ships today. v8 lets the caller name the
flat regions, from which the runtime derives

    wish = union(submitted weave rects) - union(flat rects)

and publishes it to the display processor, which switches its 3D element
per region where the hardware can (ADR-027 Decision 5).

WHERE THE FLAT LIST COMES FROM. Nowhere new: the Phase-2 draw-order plane
split already knows exactly which page quads were lifted OVER a tile and
against which tile rects. ComputeInline3dPlaneSplit now emits, from the
SAME admissions that fill |inline3d_over_clips_|, the intersection of each
admitted quad's mapped clip-applied rect with the tile rect it covers. One
pass, two lists — so they cannot disagree about which quad went where
(risk guard R12), and there is no second coordinate derivation to drift.

OPAQUE ONLY (a tightening over the design sketch). The over set admits
translucent kSrcOver content too — a scrim, a fade, a rounded-corner mask —
and there the woven 3D still shows THROUGH the 2D. Marking that flat would
switch the lens off over working 3D content, which is the one failure this
feature must never cause. The producer therefore gates on
DrawQuad::ShouldDrawWithBlending(), which covers every way a quad can be
non-opaque over the tile (needs_blending, opacity < 1, blend mode, mask
filter). A translucent quad simply leaves its area 3D = today's ghosting.

COARSENING: DROP, NEVER MERGE. The wire cap is 16
(XR_WEAVE_SUBMIT_MAX_FLAT_RECTS_DXR). Over the cap the list is reduced by
strict-containment dedupe (lossless) then largest-area-first truncation,
DISCARDING the smallest. Bounding-box merging is never done, not even for
rects that look adjacent: a flat rect that GREW past its true extent
flattens working 3D (a mono regression), whereas a MISSING one leaves that
area 3D (the already-shipped ghosting). The asymmetry is the whole rule.
The throttled plane-split marker now carries flat{raw/sent/dropped/
translucent} so a drop is visible rather than silent.

PLUMBING. The list rides SetInline3dOverPlane — the same GPU task as the
plane it describes — so the plane and the rects explaining it can never
land from different frames; the atomicity is the FIFO queue's, not a second
sync. On the GPU thread it is DRAINED at the batch (single use), so an
error path that skips a publish cannot re-send a stale wish against a later
frame's weave rects. New DisplayXRWeaveProvider::SetBatchWish carries it
into the batch bracket — deliberately NOT SetBatchOverlay, which is
reserved for handing the DP an over-plane to composite and which moves
PIXELS; this channel moves only the hardware element. The client chains
XrWeaveSubmitFlatRegionsDXR on the LAST batch chunk (the v4 overlay
precedent: one whole-window statement, landing once after every rect has
woven), spliced ahead of whatever is already chained so it composes with
the overlay chain rather than replacing it.

COMPATIBILITY. Gated on the runtime's reported weave_spec_version >= 8.
A pre-v8 runtime would skip an unknown chained struct anyway, but the
browser does not get to assume every shipped runtime walks unknown chains
politely, so the gate makes the pre-v8 wire byte-IDENTICAL rather than
merely probably-ignored. Empty list, pre-v8 runtime, macOS, or a DP with no
per-region capability all reduce to exactly the behaviour that ships today
— the wish is advisory in the ADR-027 Decision 6 / ADR-030 sense: it moves
the hardware element and nothing else, and the woven pixels are
bit-identical with and without it.

Vendored header re-synced from the runtime canonical at spec v8. v7 and v8
are purely ADDITIVE over the v6 copy it replaced (verified: no existing
XrStructureType value, struct layout or entry point changed), so a client
built against it still runs unchanged against a pre-v8 runtime, and
"Local modifications: none" stays true.

No consumer of the wish exists in this patch beyond the runtime itself;
Stage 3 (the frosted-glass compromise) builds on this plumbing.

